{
  "query": "How do I launch a lending or exchange app on Sonic without running into high gas costs?",
  "summary": "Sonic is an EVM-compatible Layer 1 from the Sonic Labs team (the successor to Fantom), so Solidity contracts and standard tooling like Hardhat or Foundry work with few changes. Its fee structure is generally low, and its Fee Monetization (FeeM) program can pay developers a share of network fees generated by their contracts. Lending and DEX protocols already active on Sonic include Silo Finance (lending), Euler (lending), SwapX and Shadow Exchange (DEXs), and Beets (liquidity and yield). Verify current deployment status and program terms in Sonic's docs before committing.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "step": 1,
      "title": "Set up an EVM development environment",
      "details": "Use Solidity with Hardhat or Foundry, point the RPC at Sonic mainnet or the Sonic testnet, and fund deployer wallets with S, Sonic's native gas token. Test everything on testnet first."
    },
    {
      "step": 2,
      "title": "Use proven lending and DEX architectures",
      "details": "For lending, study Aave-style or Compound-style pool designs and Euler's modular vault model. For exchanges, consider a concentrated-liquidity AMM like Uniswap v3 or a ve(3,3) model like Velodrome/Solidly, which Shadow and SwapX have adapted. Reusing audited code cuts both development time and gas-heavy bugs."
    },
    {
      "step": 3,
      "title": "Register for Fee Monetization (FeeM)",
      "details": "Check whether your contracts qualify for Sonic's FeeM program. If they do, registering can route a portion of the fees your contracts generate back to you, which offsets your users' gas costs and creates a revenue stream."
    },
    {
      "step": 4,
      "title": "Optimize contracts to minimize gas",
      "details": "Enable the Solidity optimizer with appropriate run counts, pack storage variables, minimize SSTORE operations, use custom errors instead of revert strings, batch user actions where possible, and favor calldata over memory for read-only arrays."
    },
    {
      "step": 5,
      "title": "Design for low-friction user onboarding",
      "details": "Bridge assets through the official Sonic bridge or supported partners, and consider account abstraction or gas sponsorship (paymasters) so new users can transact without first acquiring S."
    },
    {
      "step": 6,
      "title": "Audit, monitor, and iterate",
      "details": "Get at least one independent security audit before mainnet. Monitor gas usage per function with tools like Tenderly or Dune dashboards and optimize the most-called functions first."
    }
  ],
