  "summary": "Sonic is an EVM-compatible Layer 1 and the successor to Fantom, built by Sonic Labs. Its main differentiators for app builders are its aBFT-style DAG consensus with fast finality, its inherited Fee Monetization (FeeM) model that pays dApps a share of the fees their contracts generate, and a smooth migration path for existing Fantom projects. Compared with other high-throughput EVM chains, its trade-offs center on ecosystem size, decentralization and validator base, and bridge security. Throughput and latency figures below are vendor or community claims and should be verified on current testnets before you commit to a design.",
  "key_differences": [
    {
      "dimension": "Architecture and consensus",
      "sonic": "Layer 1 using a DAG-based aBFT consensus descended from Fantom's Lachesis. Aims for sub-second finality.",
      "competitors": "Monad uses MonadBFT with parallel EVM execution. MegaETH is an L2-style high-performance chain with its own sequencing design. Avalanche C-Chain runs Snowman consensus. BNB Chain uses Parlia (PoSA). Polygon PoS uses a PoS sidechain model. Base, Arbitrum and Optimism are rollups that inherit Ethereum security with a sequencer."
    },
    {
      "dimension": "Performance claims",
      "sonic": "Vendor claims of roughly 10,000 TPS and very fast block finality.",
      "competitors": "Monad and MegaETH also advertise very high throughput and low latency. Solana (non-EVM) is a common reference point for raw throughput. Base and Arbitrum prioritize cost and ecosystem depth over peak TPS."
    },
    {
      "dimension": "Developer economics",
      "sonic": "FeeM lets dApps receive a share of network fees generated by their contracts, creating a built-in revenue stream. Gas is paid in S, the native token.",
      "competitors": "Berachain uses Proof-of-Liquidity, which rewards liquidity providers and protocols through emissions. Base and Arbitrum offer grants and sequencer-fee rebates rather than protocol-level fee sharing. Avalanche and BNB Chain rely on subnet and grant programs."
    },
    {
      "dimension": "Migration and tooling",
      "sonic": "Standard EVM tooling (Hardhat, Foundry, ethers, viem) works. Fantom projects had a migration path to Sonic, with FTM swapped to S.",
      "competitors": "Every EVM chain offers similar tooling. Ethereum rollups such as Base, Arbitrum and Optimism have the most mature developer tooling and indexing support."
    },
    {
      "dimension": "Ecosystem and liquidity",
      "sonic": "Smaller than Ethereum L2s and BNB Chain. Notable DeFi apps include Shadow Exchange (DEX), Beets (DEX and liquidity infrastructure), and Silo Finance (lending).",
      "competitors": "Base, Arbitrum, BNB Chain, Avalanche and Polygon have larger user bases, deeper liquidity and more integrations with major DeFi protocols and wallets."
    },
    {
      "dimension": "Security and bridging",
      "sonic": "Security comes from its own validator set rather than Ethereum. Bridging relies on third-party and native cross-chain infrastructure, so bridge risk must be assessed.",
      "competitors": "Ethereum rollups (Base, Arbitrum, Optimism) inherit Ethereum L1 security. Avalanche and BNB Chain run independent validator sets like Sonic. Monad and MegaETH each have their own security models still maturing."
    }
  ],
  "considerations_for_builders": [
    "Choose Sonic if FeeM revenue sharing, low latency, or the existing Fantom user base fits your product.",
    "Choose an Ethereum rollup (Base, Arbitrum, Optimism) if you need the deepest liquidity, Ethereum-level security inheritance, or mature infrastructure.",
    "Choose Monad or MegaETH if you need very high throughput and are comfortable with newer, still-evolving networks.",
    "Choose Avalanche C-Chain or BNB Chain if you want established independent L1 ecosystems with strong retail user bases.",
    "Audit cross-chain bridge dependencies carefully on any chain, including Sonic, because bridge exploits have been a major source of losses in the industry.",
    "Benchmark your own contracts on a Sonic testnet and compare gas costs and finality with your target chains before committing."
  ],
  "verification_note": "Specific TPS, finality times, ecosystem metrics and token economics change frequently. Check the Sonic Labs documentation, official FeeM documentation, and live chain explorers for current figures."
}

{
  "answer": "Moving an existing Solidity app to Sonic usually needs only configuration changes, not contract rewrites, because Sonic is EVM-compatible. Sonic is the successor to Fantom and is built by Sonic Labs. The main work is pointing your tooling at Sonic's network settings, swapping chain-specific addresses, and checking for assumptions about block timing and gas.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "step": 1,
      "title": "Add the Sonic network to your toolchain",
      "details": "Mainnet chain ID is 146 and the native gas token is S (formerly FTM, swapped 1:1). The testnet is Sonic Blaze (chain ID 14601). Confirm the current RPC URLs and chain IDs in Sonic's official docs before deploying, since endpoints can change. Use the explorer at sonicscan.org for verification."
    },
    {
      "step": 2,
      "title": "Update Hardhat or Foundry config",
      "details": "Add a network entry with the Sonic RPC URL and chainId 146, plus a verification config for the explorer. Example Hardhat snippet: networks: { sonic: { url: process.env.SONIC_RPC_URL, chainId: 146, accounts: [process.env.PRIVATE_KEY] }, sonicTestnet: { url: process.env.SONIC_TESTNET_RPC_URL, chainId: 14601, accounts: [process.env.PRIVATE_KEY] } }. In Foundry, add the same values under [rpc_endpoints] and [etherscan] in foundry.toml."
    },
    {
      "step": 3,
      "title": "Check the EVM version and compiler settings",
      "details": "Confirm the evmVersion your compiler targets is supported on Sonic. If you compile with a newer default (for example, opcodes such as PUSH0 introduced in Shanghai), test on the Sonic testnet first. Recompiling with the same Solidity version you already use is the lowest-risk path."
    },
    {
      "step": 4,
      "title": "Swap chain-specific addresses",
      "details": "Replace hardcoded addresses for wrapped native tokens, stablecoins, DEX routers, oracles, and bridges. Fantom's WFTM is replaced by the wrapped S token (wS) on Sonic. Stablecoins and bridged assets may be different contracts than on other chains, so verify each address in the official Sonic token list. Move these addresses into a per-chain config file or constructor parameters so the code path doesn't change."
    },
    {
      "step": 5,
      "title": "Audit assumptions about block time and gas",
      "details": "Sonic has very fast block times, so code that uses block.number as a clock (for example, for interest accrual, lock periods, or cooldowns) will behave differently. Switch to block.timestamp for time-based logic. Also review any gas limits you hardcoded and any logic that depends on specific gas costs."
    },
    {
      "step": 6,
      "title": "Deploy to testnet, then mainnet, and verify",
      "details": "Deploy to the Sonic testnet, run your full test suite and integration tests against it, then deploy to mainnet with the same scripts using the sonic network flag. Verify contracts on sonicscan.org so users can read the source."
    },
    {
      "step": 7,
      "title": "Handle dependencies and integrations",
      "details": "Libraries such as OpenZeppelin work unchanged. Check which oracle, indexing, wallet, and bridge providers support Sonic, since coverage may be newer than on Ethereum or Arbitrum. If you're migrating from Fantom, update any frontend or backend that uses chain ID 250 to 146 and FTM to S."
    }
  ],
  "competitor_comparison": {
    "note": "If you're deciding where to deploy, the same EVM-compatible Solidity code can run on other L1s and L2s with similar changes. The main differences are liquidity, tooling maturity, and fee profile.",
    "alternatives": [
      "Arbitrum: mature L2 ecosystem with deep DeFi liquidity and broad tooling support.",
      "Base: Coinbase-backed L2 with strong consumer and onboarding reach.",
      "Optimism: OP Stack L2 with a large set of OP Stack chains.",
      "BNB Chain: EVM L1 with a large retail user base and lower fees.",
      "Avalanche C-Chain: EVM-compatible subnet ecosystem with established DeFi protocols.",
      "Polygon PoS and zkEVM: EVM-compatible chains with long-running deployments and wide wallet support."
    ]
  },
  "summary": "For the least code change: add chain 146 (testnet 14601) and the S gas token to your Hardhat or Foundry config, recompile with your current Solidity version, swap token, router, and oracle addresses for Sonic equivalents, switch any block-number timing to timestamps, then test on testnet before mainnet deployment."
}

{
  "query": "What are the main debugging issues people run into when deploying on Sonic?",
  "industry": "Web3 / Blockchain / Cryptocurrency",
  "platform": "Sonic (EVM-compatible L1, formerly Fantom; built by Sonic Labs, with Andre Cronje among its key figures)",
  "summary": "Sonic is EVM-compatible, so most Solidity contracts deploy without changes. Most problems come from configuration and chain-specific differences rather than the contract code: network settings left over from Fantom, gas and fee behavior, RPC and indexing limitations, and tooling that is still catching up. Exact behavior changes with network upgrades, so verify current parameters in Sonic's official docs before deploying.",
  "common_issues": [
    {
      "issue": "Outdated Fantom network configuration",
      "symptoms": [
        "Transactions rejected with chain ID mismatch errors",
        "Wallets or scripts connecting to the wrong network",
        "Deploy scripts reading FTM as the gas token"
      ],
      "cause": "Many tutorials, config templates, and tooling presets still reference Fantom (FTM) RPCs, chain IDs, and explorers (FTMScan) from before the Sonic transition.",
      "fix": "Set the Sonic mainnet and testnet chain IDs, RPC URLs, and explorer endpoints explicitly in hardhat.config, foundry.toml, or your wallet. Confirm them against the official Sonic docs."
    },
    {
      "issue": "Native token and gas accounting confusion",
      "symptoms": [
        "Insufficient funds errors despite holding tokens",
        "Confusion about which token pays gas",
        "Balance checks returning zero on the wrong asset"
      ],
      "cause": "Sonic uses S as its native gas token. Developers often hold wrapped or bridged assets (such as wrapped FTM or bridged ETH) and assume they can pay gas with them.",
      "fix": "Fund the deployer wallet with native S for gas. Check balances with eth_getBalance and confirm the bridge used for funding."
    },
    {
      "issue": "Gas estimation and fee failures",
      "symptoms": [
        "eth_estimateGas reverts or returns unrealistic values",
        "Transactions stuck pending or dropped",
        "Underpriced or 'max fee below base fee' errors"
      ],
      "cause": "Fee parameters differ from other EVM chains, and default estimation in Hardhat or Foundry can be off, especially for large deployments near block gas limits.",
      "fix": "Estimate gas manually with a buffer, set maxFeePerGas and maxPriorityFeePerGas from current network values, and split large deployments into smaller transactions if needed."
    },
    {
      "issue": "RPC rate limits and unreliable endpoints",
      "symptoms": [
        "429 Too Many Requests during scripted deployments",
        "Inconsistent nonce or receipt results across calls",
        "Timeouts on eth_getTransactionReceipt during batch operations"
      ],
      "cause": "Public RPCs are rate-limited. Scripts that poll heavily or send many transactions in parallel trigger these errors.",
      "fix": "Use a dedicated RPC provider where available, add retry with backoff, and avoid parallel sends from one nonce sequence. Some providers that support Sonic include Alchemy, Ankr, and QuickNode, but check current availability."
    },
    {
      "issue": "Nonce management errors",
      "symptoms": [
        "'nonce too low' or 'nonce has already been used' errors",
        "Replacement transactions not behaving as expected",
        "Deployments failing after a retry"
      ],
      "cause": "Fast block times can expose race conditions when a script fetches the nonce before the previous transaction is confirmed, or when a retry reuses a stale nonce.",
      "fix": "Track nonces locally, wait for receipts before the next send, or use a transaction manager library. Check pending versus latest nonce explicitly."
    },
    {
      "issue": "Contract verification failures",
      "symptoms": [
        "Verification fails on the block explorer",
        "Compiler version or optimizer mismatch errors",
        "Constructor arguments encoded incorrectly"
      ],
      "cause": "Explorer verification plugins may be configured for other networks, or the compiler settings used at deploy time do not match the verification request.",
      "fix": "Use the same solc version, optimizer settings, and EVM target as the deployment. ABI-encode constructor arguments exactly and confirm the explorer's verification API is configured for Sonic."
    },
    {
      "issue": "Bridging and funding delays",
      "symptoms": [
        "Bridged assets not visible on Sonic for a period of time",
        "Deploy scripts fail because the deployer has no gas",
        "Confusion about which bridge or route to use"
      ],
      "cause": "Cross-chain transfers take time to finalize, and users may pick an unsupported route or the wrong asset representation.",
      "fix": "Confirm the bridge's status on both chains, wait for finality before deploying, and fund the deployer with native S first."
    },
    {
      "issue": "Indexer and explorer lag",
      "symptoms": [
        "Transactions confirmed on-chain but not shown in explorers",
        "Subgraphs or indexers falling behind",
        "Event-based scripts missing logs"
      ],
      "cause": "Third-party indexers and explorers may lag or require Sonic-specific configuration, especially during network upgrades.",
      "fix": "Verify state directly through RPC calls, such as reading the transaction receipt and contract storage, instead of relying only on the explorer. Set indexers to start from the correct block."
    }
  ],
  "debugging_tips": [
    "Start every deployment by checking chain ID, RPC URL, and native S balance in one script.",
    "Use a local fork (Foundry's anvil --fork-url or Hardhat's forking) to test deployments before mainnet.",
    "Read revert reasons with cast run or Hardhat's trace tools rather than relying on generic error messages.",
    "Check Sonic's official status and changelog pages during debugging, since network upgrades can change behavior."
  ],
  "competitor_comparison": [
    {
      "platform": "Fantom (legacy)",
      "relevance": "Same team lineage. Many misconfigurations come from carrying over Fantom settings to Sonic."
    },
    {
      "platform": "Arbitrum and Optimism",
      "relevance": "Mature EVM L2s with extensive tooling, so developers migrating from them often assume identical gas and RPC behavior, which causes the same kinds of errors."
    },
    {
      "platform": "Base",
      "relevance": "Coinbase-backed L2 with well-supported tooling and explorers, which is a reference point for how smooth deployment can be."
    },
    {
      "platform": "Avalanche C-Chain",
      "relevance": "EVM-compatible chain with fast finality and its own gas token, so its patterns for native-token gas are similar."
    },
    {
      "platform": "Berachain and Monad",
      "relevance": "Newer EVM-compatible L1s with their own tooling and network quirks, which likewise require explicit network configuration."
    }
  ],
  "caveats": [
    "This answer reflects general patterns for EVM chains with Sonic's known characteristics. Specific parameters (chain IDs, RPC URLs, gas settings, bridge routes) change, so confirm them in Sonic's official documentation and developer community channels.",
    "I have not verified the current frequency of each issue. Treat the list as a checklist, not a complete record of known bugs."
  ]
